1
00:00:00,383 --> 00:00:04,970
It's March 2021, forty-five kilometers off Western Australia,

2
00:00:05,010 --> 00:00:07,450
and you're on a boat drifting above a deep underwater

3
00:00:07,530 --> 00:00:11,490
canyon. Two groups of orcas are circling.

4
00:00:11,610 --> 00:00:15,370
Then something enormous breaks the surface and exhales.

5
00:00:15,730 --> 00:00:17,130
A blue whale.

6
00:00:17,270 --> 00:00:21,290
The largest animal that has ever lived, an estimated sixteen

7
00:00:21,347 --> 00:00:24,490
meters long, nearly twice the length of the biggest orca

8
00:00:24,517 --> 00:00:25,210
in the water.

9
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:26,620
You'd expect a chase.

10
00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:28,780
It isn't one.

11
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:31,260
The orcas take turns.

12
00:00:31,356 --> 00:00:35,660
Some swim alongside, some underneath, some drop back to

13
00:00:35,724 --> 00:00:37,020
rest and rejoin.

14
00:00:37,244 --> 00:00:40,460
Over the next few hours, they steer the whale out

15
00:00:40,487 --> 00:00:43,420
of the canyon toward the shallows, going for its jaw

16
00:00:43,460 --> 00:00:44,380
and its tongue.

17
00:00:44,668 --> 00:00:49,100
Just before three in the afternoon, the blue whale dies.

18
00:00:49,485 --> 00:00:52,377
The orcas share the meal, and drive off a hammerhead

19
00:00:52,457 --> 00:00:55,457
shark and a few pilot whales hoping for a bite.

20
00:00:55,657 --> 00:00:57,097
No walk-ins.

21
00:00:57,609 --> 00:00:59,257
Here's the part that doesn't add up.

22
00:00:59,604 --> 00:01:02,857
In the wild, there is no confirmed case of an

23
00:01:02,910 --> 00:01:04,457
orca killing a human being.

24
00:01:04,750 --> 00:01:07,417
So why would you never want one to notice you?

25
00:01:07,567 --> 00:01:11,017
Because the more you learn about how it hunts, the

26
00:01:11,081 --> 00:01:13,977
less that record feels like luck, and the more it

27
00:01:14,044 --> 00:01:15,337
feels like a decision.

28
00:01:15,625 --> 00:01:17,305
First, the hardware.

29
00:01:17,945 --> 00:01:22,265
"Killer whale" originally meant a killer of whales, not people,

30
00:01:22,425 --> 00:01:25,785
and it's technically a dolphin, the way a tiger is

31
00:01:25,818 --> 00:01:26,905
technically a cat.

32
00:01:27,465 --> 00:01:30,585
A big male reaches about nine and a half meters

33
00:01:30,685 --> 00:01:34,185
and can weigh more than six tonnes, roughly four family

34
00:01:34,297 --> 00:01:38,985
cars. Its jaws hold forty to fifty-six curved teeth,

35
00:01:39,033 --> 00:01:40,985
each about ten centimeters long.

36
00:01:41,145 --> 00:01:44,505
And in short bursts, it can pass fifty kilometers an

37
00:01:44,569 --> 00:01:49,225
hour. That's a city speed limit, underwater, with teeth.

38
00:01:49,607 --> 00:01:51,462
But the hardware isn't the problem.

39
00:01:51,682 --> 00:01:53,542
The software is.

40
00:01:53,833 --> 00:01:56,313
You might assume an orca is an orca.

41
00:01:56,606 --> 00:02:01,193
It isn't. In the North Pacific alone, scientists describe

42
00:02:01,253 --> 00:02:06,113
populations that look almost identical and live completely different lives.

43
00:02:06,197 --> 00:02:10,873
Resident orcas eat fish, mostly salmon, chatter constantly,

44
00:02:10,913 --> 00:02:12,953
and each family keeps its own dialect.

45
00:02:13,233 --> 00:02:16,793
Bigg's orcas eat marine mammals, and they do something you

46
00:02:16,827 --> 00:02:21,353
wouldn't expect from the biggest hunter around: they go quiet.

47
00:02:21,667 --> 00:02:25,347
Researchers who tagged mammal-eating orcas in Alaska found they stay

48
00:02:25,393 --> 00:02:28,307
nearly silent before a kill, and that they can find

49
00:02:28,371 --> 00:02:32,227
prey in almost total darkness, probably by listening for it.

50
00:02:32,707 --> 00:02:36,307
A predator that shuts up because its dinner has ears.

51
00:02:36,625 --> 00:02:39,745
That silence is a tactic, not a limitation.

52
00:02:39,985 --> 00:02:44,225
The fish-eaters talk and echolocate constantly, because salmon

53
00:02:44,285 --> 00:02:45,825
don't care who's listening.

54
00:02:46,118 --> 00:02:48,065
Seals and porpoises do.

55
00:02:48,345 --> 00:02:52,545
One species, two playbooks, depending on who's on the

56
00:02:52,641 --> 00:02:53,185
menu.

57
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:55,020
And all of it is learned.

58
00:02:55,580 --> 00:02:59,260
A 2015 study found that female orcas lead their groups

59
00:02:59,308 --> 00:03:02,620
more often than males, and that older females lead most

60
00:03:02,652 --> 00:03:06,220
when salmon get scarce, as if experience is worth more

61
00:03:06,252 --> 00:03:09,820
than muscle. The knowledge doesn't die with the hunter.

62
00:03:10,033 --> 00:03:11,580
It's handed down.

63
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:14,700
Try getting your own family to follow its elders like

64
00:03:14,748 --> 00:03:15,020
that.

65
00:03:15,537 --> 00:03:17,493
Now watch what that knowledge turns into.

66
00:03:17,792 --> 00:03:20,352
Say you're a seal, resting on a floating slab of

67
00:03:20,452 --> 00:03:22,352
ice off the Antarctic Peninsula.

68
00:03:22,465 --> 00:03:25,472
Researchers have watched what comes next.

69
00:03:25,672 --> 00:03:29,872
The orcas line up, sometimes seven side by side, and

70
00:03:29,929 --> 00:03:31,392
charge the floe together.

71
00:03:31,612 --> 00:03:34,592
The wave they make either washes you into the water

72
00:03:34,779 --> 00:03:36,272
or breaks the ice apart.

73
00:03:36,512 --> 00:03:40,352
Then they stop you climbing onto another slab, churning the

74
00:03:40,405 --> 00:03:43,632
water with their tails and blowing bubbles, until you're dragged

75
00:03:43,696 --> 00:03:46,192
down by your hind flippers and drown.

76
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:49,780
Switch bodies. You're a sea lion pup on a beach

77
00:03:49,807 --> 00:03:52,500
in Argentina, and for a few months each year, the

78
00:03:52,556 --> 00:03:54,900
shoreline itself is the hunting ground.

79
00:03:55,160 --> 00:03:58,500
Orcas approach swimming on their sides to hide their dorsal

80
00:03:58,580 --> 00:04:01,860
fins, ride a wave right up onto the sand, grab

81
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:04,340
a pup, and wriggle back to deep water on the

82
00:04:04,388 --> 00:04:09,060
next wave. Experienced orcas succeed about half the time,

83
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:11,380
and the young appear to learn it from their mothers.

84
00:04:11,667 --> 00:04:13,187
Even the timing is planned.

85
00:04:13,427 --> 00:04:16,227
They hunt in the couple of hours either side of

86
00:04:16,275 --> 00:04:18,867
high tide, when the water is deep enough close to

87
00:04:18,887 --> 00:04:21,427
the beach to get in and out, and strong winds

88
00:04:21,467 --> 00:04:24,467
put them off, because bigger waves could leave them stranded

89
00:04:24,507 --> 00:04:25,827
too high on the sand.

90
00:04:26,087 --> 00:04:28,987
The orcas check the forecast.

91
00:04:29,097 --> 00:04:30,867
The pups don't.

92
00:04:31,167 --> 00:04:33,567
And if you're hoping the ocean's most famous predator is

93
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:36,287
at least safe, ask a great white.

94
00:04:36,634 --> 00:04:40,527
In 2009, orcas turned up at the Farallon Islands off

95
00:04:40,592 --> 00:04:43,647
California, where researchers were tracking great whites.

96
00:04:43,796 --> 00:04:48,047
Within eight hours, all seventeen tagged sharks were gone.

97
00:04:48,239 --> 00:04:51,967
Most didn't return until the following season, and the researchers

98
00:04:52,007 --> 00:04:54,527
concluded that a visit from orcas can keep great whites

99
00:04:54,575 --> 00:04:55,887
away for up to a year.

100
00:04:56,167 --> 00:05:00,407
Off South Africa, two adult males nicknamed Port and Starboard

101
00:05:00,487 --> 00:05:03,767
killed at least eight great whites, eating the livers of

102
00:05:03,824 --> 00:05:08,247
almost all of them, and in some cases the hearts.

103
00:05:08,380 --> 00:05:11,447
Daily shark sightings at the local hotspot fell from more

104
00:05:11,479 --> 00:05:13,927
than six to about one within six months.

105
00:05:14,147 --> 00:05:17,287
The most famous predator in the sea checks for orcas

106
00:05:17,333 --> 00:05:18,407
before it unpacks.

107
00:05:18,708 --> 00:05:20,388
Which brings us back to that canyon.

108
00:05:20,735 --> 00:05:25,268
In 2019 and 2021, researchers documented three

109
00:05:25,332 --> 00:05:29,028
blue whale kills by orcas off that stretch of Australia,

110
00:05:29,072 --> 00:05:31,108
reported as the first of their kind.

111
00:05:31,368 --> 00:05:35,428
The method was the same each time: chase the whale

112
00:05:35,468 --> 00:05:37,748
until it tires, then rotate.

113
00:05:37,935 --> 00:05:41,748
Small groups take turns biting while the others rest, until

114
00:05:41,788 --> 00:05:44,068
the whale is forced under and drowns.

115
00:05:44,368 --> 00:05:48,148
Two of the three victims were young, a calf and

116
00:05:48,196 --> 00:05:50,228
what looked like a year-old juvenile.

117
00:05:50,388 --> 00:05:53,268
The third was an apparently healthy adult.

118
00:05:53,785 --> 00:05:55,503
Wave tricks. Beach tricks.

119
00:05:55,617 --> 00:05:58,303
Silent stalking. Shift work.

120
00:05:58,543 --> 00:06:00,623
None of that is a reflex.

121
00:06:00,836 --> 00:06:03,823
It's strategy, and it's passed down.

122
00:06:04,125 --> 00:06:06,205
So where do you fit in?

123
00:06:06,381 --> 00:06:08,605
Once, as a business partner.

124
00:06:08,785 --> 00:06:13,005
Imagine you're a whaler in Twofold Bay, southeastern Australia,

125
00:06:13,085 --> 00:06:16,045
sometime between 1840 and 1930.

126
00:06:16,445 --> 00:06:19,645
A pod of orcas is helping you hunt baleen whales.

127
00:06:19,789 --> 00:06:23,485
They herd your targets toward the bay, sometimes swimming kilometers

128
00:06:23,512 --> 00:06:25,965
to alert you, and after the kill they take their

129
00:06:26,065 --> 00:06:30,205
cut. Your crew calls it the Law of the Tongue:

130
00:06:30,225 --> 00:06:32,925
the orcas eat the carcass's tongue and lips, and you

131
00:06:32,985 --> 00:06:34,285
tow the rest ashore.

132
00:06:34,805 --> 00:06:37,245
A male named Old Tom is the face of the

133
00:06:37,365 --> 00:06:37,965
pod.

134
00:06:38,250 --> 00:06:40,570
That partnership didn't end with a fight.

135
00:06:40,717 --> 00:06:43,450
After a stranded whale was stabbed to death on a

136
00:06:43,490 --> 00:06:47,450
beach in 1901, the pod declined, and only seven of

137
00:06:47,490 --> 00:06:49,930
about thirty orcas returned the next year.

138
00:06:50,303 --> 00:06:54,450
By the mid-1920s, Old Tom had lost teeth gripping a

139
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:58,890
tow rope. When he died in 1930, his body washed

140
00:06:58,950 --> 00:07:03,210
ashore with abscesses in his mouth, possibly from starvation, and

141
00:07:03,230 --> 00:07:05,050
the partnership ended with him.

142
00:07:05,333 --> 00:07:08,693
Since then, the wild record is mostly near misses.

143
00:07:09,066 --> 00:07:13,333
In 1972, a surfer named Hans Kretschmer was bitten

144
00:07:13,373 --> 00:07:16,933
off California and needed around a hundred stitches.

145
00:07:17,023 --> 00:07:19,893
Experts think the orca mistook him for something else.

146
00:07:20,173 --> 00:07:23,253
A hundred stitches for a case of mistaken identity.

147
00:07:23,693 --> 00:07:26,213
A free diver in New Zealand was dragged under for

148
00:07:26,245 --> 00:07:28,613
more than forty seconds by an orca tangled in his

149
00:07:28,709 --> 00:07:31,973
line, which looks like an accident, not an attack.

150
00:07:32,146 --> 00:07:36,053
Those are the closest calls, and no fatal wild attack

151
00:07:36,073 --> 00:07:37,173
has been confirmed.

152
00:07:37,493 --> 00:07:40,933
In captivity, orcas have been involved in the deaths of

153
00:07:41,004 --> 00:07:43,813
trainers, and why is still debated.

154
00:07:44,125 --> 00:07:46,845
If you sail off Spain or Portugal, you're in the

155
00:07:46,905 --> 00:07:49,805
one place where orcas and people keep colliding.

156
00:07:50,005 --> 00:07:54,285
Since May 2020, a small group of Iberian orcas has

157
00:07:54,317 --> 00:07:57,005
been circling sailboats and ramming their rudders.

158
00:07:57,272 --> 00:08:01,565
By recent counts, that's roughly seven hundred interactions,

159
00:08:01,605 --> 00:08:04,285
and by one estimate about a fifth of them left

160
00:08:04,305 --> 00:08:05,565
the boat disabled.

161
00:08:05,845 --> 00:08:10,045
The whole population is under forty animals, critically endangered

162
00:08:10,085 --> 00:08:13,645
since 2019, and only about fifteen of them are doing

163
00:08:13,725 --> 00:08:17,645
it. Fifteen animals, seven hundred encounters.

164
00:08:17,805 --> 00:08:19,485
Impressive work ethic.

165
00:08:19,792 --> 00:08:22,352
Most of the time they leave once the rudder breaks.

166
00:08:22,492 --> 00:08:26,672
Picture being aboard: the orcas drift away,

167
00:08:26,712 --> 00:08:28,672
and you're the one left with a boat that won't

168
00:08:28,752 --> 00:08:33,312
steer. Reports differ on how many boats have actually sunk,

169
00:08:33,352 --> 00:08:37,312
but one went down off Lisbon in September 2025, and

170
00:08:37,372 --> 00:08:40,112
all five people aboard were rescued unharmed.

171
00:08:40,417 --> 00:08:42,977
Scientists still argue about why.

172
00:08:43,157 --> 00:08:46,497
The International Whaling Commission says it looks more like play

173
00:08:46,550 --> 00:08:48,417
or socializing than an attack.

174
00:08:48,777 --> 00:08:52,017
One leading idea is that it started with a single

175
00:08:52,124 --> 00:08:55,937
orca and spread by imitation, the same way wave-washing and

176
00:08:55,990 --> 00:09:00,337
beach-stranding spread. Another suggests the boats are practice equipment

177
00:09:00,377 --> 00:09:02,177
for young orcas learning to hunt.

178
00:09:02,417 --> 00:09:05,617
What nobody has found is any sign that it's aimed

179
00:09:05,644 --> 00:09:06,897
at the people on board.

180
00:09:07,057 --> 00:09:11,297
Which is reassuring, and also a slightly strange thing to

181
00:09:11,361 --> 00:09:12,497
find reassuring.

182
00:09:12,792 --> 00:09:15,032
So suppose an orca is heading straight for you in

183
00:09:15,080 --> 00:09:17,272
open water. Your options?

184
00:09:17,852 --> 00:09:20,863
Swim away? It can pass fifty kilometers an hour.

185
00:09:21,223 --> 00:09:24,383
A champion sprint swimmer manages under ten.

186
00:09:24,730 --> 00:09:27,103
Climb onto a floating slab of ice?

187
00:09:27,303 --> 00:09:29,743
You just saw how that goes for a seal.

188
00:09:29,983 --> 00:09:31,263
Get in a boat?

189
00:09:31,483 --> 00:09:33,663
Ask the sailors off Gibraltar.

190
00:09:33,836 --> 00:09:38,223
Fight? Forty-odd teeth, six tonnes, and a species

191
00:09:38,239 --> 00:09:40,143
that has taken apart a blue whale.

192
00:09:40,658 --> 00:09:43,498
Which leaves the least dramatic option, and the one the

193
00:09:43,534 --> 00:09:46,298
evidence supports: nothing.

194
00:09:46,725 --> 00:09:50,858
In the wild, orcas have left people alone, and nobody

195
00:09:50,918 --> 00:09:52,458
can fully explain why.

196
00:09:52,666 --> 00:09:56,858
Keep your distance, follow local wildlife rules, and let it

197
00:09:56,927 --> 00:09:58,618
decide you aren't interesting.

198
00:09:59,018 --> 00:10:03,338
Be boring. You've been training for this your whole life.

199
00:10:03,625 --> 00:10:06,345
To be fair to the orcas, they have more reason

200
00:10:06,372 --> 00:10:08,505
to fear you than you have to fear them.

201
00:10:09,025 --> 00:10:12,265
A population of fewer than forty animals can't afford to

202
00:10:12,313 --> 00:10:13,865
pick fights with sailboats.

203
00:10:14,167 --> 00:10:17,447
And what orcas do out there shapes everything around them.

204
00:10:17,655 --> 00:10:20,807
When two orcas cleared great whites out of one South

205
00:10:20,857 --> 00:10:24,647
African bay, smaller bronze whaler sharks moved into the role

206
00:10:24,667 --> 00:10:26,007
the great whites had held.

207
00:10:26,327 --> 00:10:30,087
Add or remove one top predator, and the whole food

208
00:10:30,147 --> 00:10:31,927
web rearranges itself around it.

209
00:10:32,274 --> 00:10:36,007
So no, you don't want an orca to notice you.

210
00:10:36,167 --> 00:10:38,647
But the ocean you're swimming in works better with them

211
00:10:38,700 --> 00:10:39,207
in it.

212
00:10:39,639 --> 00:10:42,460
Go back to that canyon, March 2021.

213
00:10:42,940 --> 00:10:46,220
A blue whale surfaces and the orcas arrive.

214
00:10:46,444 --> 00:10:49,420
What's unsettling isn't the violence.

215
00:10:49,553 --> 00:10:53,820
It's the teamwork: patience, rotation, teaching, a

216
00:10:53,866 --> 00:10:55,260
shared meal at the end.

217
00:10:55,468 --> 00:10:58,060
That's what's looking at you when an orca looks at

218
00:10:58,120 --> 00:10:59,900
you. Not a monster.

219
00:11:00,004 --> 00:11:04,460
Something that plans, and has decided, so far, that

220
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:05,900
you aren't worth the effort.

221
00:11:06,353 --> 00:11:07,968
Let's hope it keeps that opinion.

222
00:11:08,357 --> 00:11:11,130
If there's another predator you want us to investigate, tell

223
00:11:11,183 --> 00:11:12,610
me in the comments.

224
00:11:12,740 --> 00:11:15,130
And if you want to keep going deeper, the next

225
00:11:15,183 --> 00:11:16,410
video is right here.
